A||N||2||haski||wiring harness conversion||||5||
Z||000000||haski||05-17-2000||05:30 PM||||Help ... I'm in the middle of doing an engine  conversion and I've encountered a bit of a puzzle. My car is an 84 M24; the donor is (was)a 1987 Buick Park Avenue. The (almost) new engine is a rebuilt 3.8L that has not been modified in any way.<BR> My problem is that the harnesses don't match. To be more specific, the main harness is OK (it, of course, fits its own ECU) but a secondary spur is not. The secondary wiring (I haven't a clue what it's for) on the Buick ends in a big black terminal and is twice the size of the corresponding terminal on the Fiero, which is found under the console about half way between the ECU and the cigarette lighter.<BR> What to do? Anyone have any suggestions? I would appreciate all the help I can get.<BR> I hope to have the car on the road  some time in June but anticipate I will have other questions before that. This project is the first of it's kind I have tackled in about 20 years <BR>Cheers,<BR>Haski    ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Dan86gt3.8||05-18-2000||12:28 AM||||umm you will probably need to use the 3.8 ecm.  As for doing the wire mods I am not sure.  you would have to figure out what wires went to what sensors and all that.  Usually wiring harness are done by conversion people (ace & chris west) but I have seen in kit car where some place in arizona is used alott.   I am not sure of the name but if you write or call they would tell you.  hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||05-19-2000||02:11 PM||||Been through this a couple of times.<P>You will need to get the complete wiring diagrams for both the Fiero and the Park Avenue and find all of the equivalent circuits and splice them in. You will find that many colors won't match. The PA harness will not just "plug in". <P>When I swapped the 3800SC to my Ciera, it took 40 hours to combine the two wiring harnesses into one. That was most of the work. I marked all of the wires as to their circuit (TPS, B+, AC, etc.). <P>Diagrams, two harnesses, beer and a TV. That's the minimum (and yes, I know *some* people can have fun without alcohol. I just tend to have more fun.)<P>Also, it helps to drop the engine in, lay<BR>harness in place and the ID where they go. Then you can begin to route and bundle them nicely.<P>For any engine swap, the wiring is the majority of the labor.<P>Email me privately if you like.<P>Terry<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-19-2000).]||||reg||1
